🧩 One thing this plan doesn't cover: how do the hosts actually TALK to each other?
Checked this against the real repo before adding it, not just vibes on top of the table above. Here's what's actually true right now:

data-net (where postgres + redis live) is internal: true in docker-compose.core.yml — it has ZERO route out of the host today. Not "unpublished," not "localhost-only" — fully sealed.

Neither postgres nor redis publish a ports: block at all right now — nothing outside their own Docker network can reach them, not even curl localhost:5432 from the laptop itself.

So "move Postgres to Oracle Cloud" isn't a docker compose -f ... up -d away — it's a real networking migration first.

✅ My idea: Tailscale mesh, skip the Docker Swarm question above
Way fewer moving parts than Swarm/overlay networks, and there's zero Swarm anywhere in this repo today to build on top of:

Install Tailscale on the laptop + the Oracle VM — free tier covers this easily, no credit card, ~5 min setup. Each machine gets a private 100.x.x.x IP that only your own devices can reach. No DB port ever touches the public internet.

On Oracle, bind Postgres/Redis to the Tailscale interface instead of leaving them Docker-internal-only.

On the laptop, point every consumer at Oracle's Tailscale IP.

⚠️ The gotcha that WILL bite you if you skip this part: there are two separate env-var families for both DB and Redis in this repo — HYPERCODE_DB_URL/DATABASE_URL and HYPERCODE_REDIS_URL/REDIS_URL. hypercode-core/celery-worker read the HYPERCODE_* ones; 36 other files read DATABASE_URL; 91 files read REDIS_URL. This is the exact trap that caused a missed container during the Aug 22 Postgres password rotation. Don't hand-edit 127 files when you do this move — introduce one POSTGRES_HOST / REDIS_HOST var in .env that all four URL vars interpolate from, so a host migration becomes a one-line .env change forever after, not a repo-wide sweep every time.

Also: re-run agents/hyperhealth/seed_checks.py --force after any DB host move. Its postgres-db-health check stores the full DSN (password included) as a literal string in its own DB row, not read live from .env — it'll keep pinging the old host/password until re-seeded. Bit us for real on the Aug 22 rotation, will bite again here if forgotten.

🚨 Real flaw in the Tier 1 list above: don't move Healer Agent to Oracle
Healer Agent's whole job is watching and restarting your laptop's containers via docker-socket-proxy-healer — a scoped Docker proxy (never mounts the raw socket directly, that's a hard rule in this repo) that only exists local to whatever host it's healing. Move Healer Agent to Oracle and it loses the ability to see or restart anything on your laptop — its entire purpose breaks. throttle-agent shares that same proxy, same problem.

Keep both healer-agent and throttle-agent on the laptop tier. Only move things to Oracle that don't need Docker API access — DB, Redis, observability, broski-bot are all genuinely fine there.